title: Electricity & Magnetism
subject: Physics
level: GCSE
emoji: 🔌
description: Circuits, current, resistance, mains electricity and magnets.
---
Charge flow formula? :: Charge = current × time (Q = It)
What is the unit of current? :: Ampere (A).
What is the unit of potential difference? :: Volt (V).
What is the unit of resistance? :: Ohm (Ω).
Ohm's law? :: Potential difference = current × resistance (V = IR)
Electrical power formulae? :: P = IV and P = I²R
Energy transferred formula for electrical appliances? :: E = P × t (or E = QV)
How are ammeters connected in a circuit? :: In series.
How are voltmeters connected? :: In parallel across the component.
In a series circuit, how does current behave? :: It is the same at all points.
In a parallel circuit, how does potential difference behave? :: It is the same across each branch.
Total resistance of resistors in series? :: The sum of each resistance.
What happens to the resistance of a thermistor as temperature rises? :: It decreases.
What happens to the resistance of an LDR as light increases? :: It decreases.
What does a diode do? :: Allows current in one direction only.
Which wire in a UK plug is the live wire? :: Brown.
Which wire in a UK plug is the neutral wire? :: Blue.
Which wire in a UK plug is the earth wire? :: Green and yellow stripes.
What is the UK mains voltage and frequency? :: 230 V and 50 Hz.
What does a fuse do? :: Melts if the current is too high, breaking the circuit.
What do like poles of magnets do? :: Repel (opposite poles attract).
What is the right-hand rule used for? :: Finding the direction of the magnetic field around a current-carrying wire.
What is a transformer's purpose? :: Changes the voltage of an alternating current (step-up or step-down).
Why is electricity transmitted at high voltage? :: To reduce current and so reduce energy lost as heat in the cables.
